To achieve a multi-region disaster recovery setup with the desired RTO and RPO, we'll need to implement a highly available and resilient architecture. Here's a high-level approach:

1. **Containerized Microservices**: Use a container orchestration platform like Kubernetes to manage and scale your microservices across multiple regions. This will ensure that your application can continue to operate even if one region becomes unavailable.

2. **Relational Database**: Use a managed database service that supports multi-region replication, such as Amazon Aurora or Google Cloud SQL. This will allow you to maintain a primary database in one region and a standby database in another region. You can use automated failover to switch to the standby database in case of a regional outage.

3. **Redis Cache Layer**: Use a managed Redis service that supports multi-region replication, such as Amazon ElastiCache or Google Cloud Memorystore. This will allow you to maintain a primary Redis instance in one region and a standby instance in another region. You can use automated failover to switch to the standby Redis instance in case of a regional outage.

4. **Object Storage Bucket**: Use a managed object storage service that supports multi-region replication, such as Amazon S3 or Google Cloud Storage. This will allow you to maintain a primary bucket in one region and a standby bucket in another region. You can use automated failover to switch to the standby bucket in case of a regional outage.

5. **Search Engine Index**: Use a managed search service that supports multi-region replication, such as Amazon OpenSearch Service or Google Cloud Search. This will allow you to maintain a primary search index in one region and a standby index in another region. You can use automated failover to switch to the standby index in case of a regional outage.

6. **Network and Load Balancing**: Use a global load balancer, such as Amazon Route 53 or Google Cloud Load Balancing, to route traffic to the appropriate region based on availability and proximity to the user. This will ensure that users are directed to the nearest healthy region.

7. **Monitoring and Alerts**: Implement monitoring and alerting for all components of your architecture using a service like Amazon CloudWatch or Google Cloud Monitoring. This will allow you to quickly detect and respond to issues in any region.

Here's a sample Terraform configuration to get you started with a multi-region setup for your microservices, database, and Redis cache:

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
  alias  = "us-east-1"
}

provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
  alias  = "us-west-2"
}

resource "aws_eks_cluster" "primary" {
  name     = "primary-cluster"
  role_arn = aws_iam_role.eks_role.arn

  provider = aws.us-east-1
}

resource "aws_eks_cluster" "secondary" {
  name     = "secondary-cluster"
  role_arn = aws_iam_role.eks_role.arn

  provider = aws.us-west-2
}

resource "aws_route53_record" "primary" {
  name    = "primary.example.com"
  type    = "A"
  ttl     = "300"
  zone_id = "ZONE_ID"

  alias {
    name   = "primary-cluster.example.com"
    zone_id = aws_route53_zone.example_com.zone_id
    evaluate_target_health = true
  }
}

resource "aws_route53_record" "secondary" {
  name    = "secondary.example.com"
  type    = "A"
  ttl     = "300"
  zone_id = "ZONE_ID"

  alias {
    name   = "secondary-cluster.example.com"
    zone_id = aws_route53_zone.example_com.zone_id
    evaluate_target_health = true
  }
}

resource "aws_route53_health_check" "primary" {
  fqdn      = aws_route53_record.primary.name
  port      = 443
  type      = "HTTPS"
  resource_path = "/"
}

resource "aws_route53_health_check" "secondary" {
  fqdn      = aws_route53_record.secondary.name
  port      = 443
  type      = "HTTPS"
  resource_path = "/"
}

resource "aws_route53_record" "failover" {
  name    = "example.com"
  type    = "A"
  ttl     = "300"
  zone_id = "ZONE_ID"

  failover_routing_policy {
    primary_routing_policy {
      health_check_id = aws_route53_health_check.primary.id
    }
    secondary_routing_policy {
      health_check_id = aws_route53_health_check.secondary.id
    }
  }
}
```

This Terraform configuration sets up two EKS clusters in different regions, along with Route 53 records and health checks to route traffic to the healthy region. You'll need to adapt this to your specific use case and add the other components (database, Redis, object storage, search engine) as needed.

